Real-world implementation: Student eligibility
public class Eligibility {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int marks = 75;
        int attendance = 85;

        if (marks >= 40 && attendance >= 75) {

            System.out.println("Student is eligible");

        } else {

            System.out.println("Student is not eligible");

        }
    }
}


Execution flow:
1. Marks are 75.
2. Attendance is 85.
3. Check whether marks are greater than or equal to 40.
4. Check whether attendance is greater than or equal to 75.
5. Both conditions are true.
6. Print eligibility confirmation.


1.6 Control statements
Control statements decide which statements execute and how many times they execute.
A. if-else
int age = 20;

if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Eligible to vote");
} else {
    System.out.println("Not eligible");
}


B. switch
int choice = 2;

switch (choice) {

    case 1:
        System.out.println("Add Student");
        break;

    case 2:
        System.out.println("View Student");
        break;

    case 3:
        System.out.println("Delete Student");
        break;

    default:
        System.out.println("Invalid choice");
}


Real-world use: Menu-driven applications, banking operations and management systems.
C. Loops
For loop:
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {

    System.out.println(i);

}


While loop:
int i = 1;

while (i <= 5) {

    System.out.println(i);

    i++;
}


Do-while loop:
int i = 1;

do {

    System.out.println(i);

    i++;

} while (i <= 5);


Difference:
- for: Best when the number of iterations is known.
- while: Best when repetition depends on a condition.
- do-while: Executes at least once.
1.7 Methods in Java
Definition: A method is a block of code that performs a particular task. It helps us reuse logic instead of writing the same code repeatedly.

Real-world example:

In a banking application, you might have:
- deposit()
- withdraw()
- checkBalance()
- transferMoney()
Syntax
returnType methodName(parameters) {

    // statements

    return value;
}


Implementation
public class Calculator {

    static int add(int a, int b) {

        return a + b;

    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int result = add(10, 20);

        System.out.println(result);

    }
}


Output:
30


How this works
1. JVM starts execution from main().
2. main() calls add(10, 20).
3. Values are passed into parameters a and b.
4. The method calculates a + b.
5. It returns 30.
6. The result is stored in result.
Important: Understand parameters, arguments, return types, static methods and instance methods.
